     1                                  ; UMBSC.SYS -- an upper memory block manager that uses NO conventional memory
     2                                  ;
     3                                  ; StevenC & Claude, 2026.  Public domain, like the work it comes from:
     4                                  ;   USE!UMBS.SYS  Marco van Zwetselaar, 1991, dedicated to the public domain
     5                                  ;   v2.1/v2.2     Krister Nordvall's NASM rewrite (PMUMB.SYS.asm in the
     6                                  ;                 ISA-PicoMEM tree is the same code with a PicoMEM check)
     7                                  ;
     8                                  ; Assemble:  nasm -f bin -o UMBSC.SYS UMBSC.ASM
     9                                  ;
    10                                  ; Use it exactly like USE!UMBS.SYS -- same command line, same answers:
    11                                  ;
    12                                  ;   DOS=UMB
    13                                  ;   DEVICE=C:\DRIVERS\UMBSC.SYS C800-D000 D800-E000
    14                                  ;
    15                                  ; WHAT IS DIFFERENT
    16                                  ;
    17                                  ; USE!UMBS keeps its XMS entry point, its INT 2Fh hook and its list of free
    18                                  ; blocks in the driver's own memory, which DOS loads in CONVENTIONAL memory
    19                                  ; because there is no upper memory yet -- 224 bytes on the V30, for the
    20                                  ; whole session, for code that runs a handful of times during boot.
    21                                  ;
    22                                  ; This one copies those ~125 bytes to the END of the last upper memory
    23                                  ; block, hands DOS the rest of that block, and then tells DOS to discard the
    24                                  ; driver: it clears bit 15 of its attribute word and reports zero units,
    25                                  ; which makes DOS treat it as a block device that found no drives and free
    26                                  ; it (MS-DOS SYSCONF.ASM, ISBLOCK -> Erase_Dev_do; no message is printed).
    27                                  ; The upper memory it keeps is the memory DOS would have given away, so
    28                                  ; nothing that was usable before is lost except those ~9 paragraphs of UMB.
    29                                  ;
    30                                  ; What callers see is unchanged: INT 2Fh AX=4300h returns 80h, AX=4310h
    31                                  ; returns the entry point, and the entry point answers function 10h
    32                                  ; (request UMB) exactly as v2.2 does, everything else as v2.2 does, or --
    33                                  ; if an XMS driver without UMB support was already loaded -- by passing the
    34                                  ; call on to it.
    35                                  
    36                                  		cpu	8086
    37                                  
    38                                  TAB		equ	9
    39                                  CR		equ	13
    40                                  LF		equ	10
    41                                  SPACE		equ	32
    42                                  MAXRANGES	equ	16
    43                                  
    44                                  rhCommand	equ	2
    45                                  rhStatus	equ	3
    46                                  rhUnits		equ	13
    47                                  rhBreak		equ	14
    48                                  rhCmdLine	equ	18
    49                                  
    50                                  		section	.text
    51                                  		org	0
    52                                  
